---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
import { SEMANTIC_PACKAGE_MANIFEST } from '@/lib/spdtf-workspace.mjs';
---

<Layout title="Six-part semantic package" description="How the business glossary, data dictionary, taxonomies, controlled vocabularies, resources and relationships form one synchronized SPDTF 2.0 package.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF 2.0" variant="brand" updated="Versioned semantic package · six distinct outputs" />
  <h1>The six-part semantic package</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Each group reviews six related kinds of content. Together they form one versioned
    agreement about meaning. They are not one taxonomy, six separate standards, or six
    files that may drift independently.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--key">
    <p class="callout__label">Versioned package contract</p>
    <p><code>{SEMANTIC_PACKAGE_MANIFEST.id}</code> · version <code>{SEMANTIC_PACKAGE_MANIFEST.version}</code> · {SEMANTIC_PACKAGE_MANIFEST.status}.</p>
    <p>{SEMANTIC_PACKAGE_MANIFEST.synchronizationRule}</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="outputs">Six distinct outputs</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="Six reviewable outputs surround one versioned semantic package. Updating a meaning in one output triggers alignment checks across the other five; none is allowed to become a competing authority.">
flowchart TB
  accTitle: The six synchronized outputs of an SPDTF semantic package
  accDescr: One versioned package connects the business glossary, data dictionary, taxonomies, controlled vocabularies, resources and relationships.
  Package["Versioned semantic package"]:::process
  Glossary["Business glossary"]:::user
  Dictionary["Data dictionary"]:::data
  Taxonomy["Taxonomies"]:::service
  Vocabulary["Controlled vocabularies"]:::service
  Resources["Resources"]:::infra
  Relationships["Relationships"]:::security
  Glossary --> Package
  Dictionary --> Package
  Taxonomy --> Package
  Vocabulary --> Package
  Resources --> Package
  Relationships --> Package
  </Diagram>
  <table>
    <caption>What each output helps a participant review</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Output</th><th scope="col">Question it answers</th><th scope="col">Typical review material</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Business glossary</th><td>What do practitioners mean by each term?</td><td>Definitions, examples, scope notes, synonyms and ownership.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Data dictionary</th><td>What data is recorded, with what expectations?</td><td>Elements, definitions, value expectations, provenance and stewardship.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Taxonomies</th><td>How are concepts organised into broader and narrower meanings?</td><td>Reviewed hierarchical organisations of domain concepts.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Controlled vocabularies</th><td>Which governed labels, codes and values may be used?</td><td>Concepts, definitions, codes, schemes and release status.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Resources</th><td>What identifiable things and concepts need stable meaning?</td><td>Classes, identifiers, descriptions, semantic homes and provenance.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Relationships</th><td>How do resources connect or participate?</td><td>Named links, roles, qualification, scope and business interpretation.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="worked">One concept across all six outputs</h2>
  <p>
    Consider the idea of a <em>legal title</em>. The glossary explains the term in
    practitioner language. The data dictionary connects recorded title identifiers and
    source fields to that meaning. A taxonomy may place title-related concepts within a
    reviewed subject structure. A controlled vocabulary may govern title status values.
    The resource model identifies <em>Legal title</em> as a kind of thing. Relationships
    connect it to a property, holder and source evidence.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Example alignment record for legal title</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Output</th><th scope="col">Example record</th><th scope="col">Alignment check</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Glossary</th><td>Definition, included/excluded cases, source and owner.</td><td>Does the definition match the resource and relationship use?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Dictionary</th><td>Title number, title status and recorded source elements.</td><td>Does every element describe the intended concept or relationship?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Taxonomy</th><td>Reviewed subject placement for title information.</td><td>Is broader/narrower structure being confused with class inheritance?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Vocabulary</th><td>Governed title-status concepts and codes.</td><td>Is each value a SKOS concept rather than a class?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Resource</th><td>Stable identifier and class description for Legal title.</td><td>Are identity and semantic home explicit?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Relationship</th><td>registered over Property; evidenced by Source record.</td><td>Are direction, role and qualification correct?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="synchronisation">Synchronisation and ownership</h2>
  <p>
    The package manifest names the maintained source for each output, its semantic owner,
    candidate version and derivation. A change to a definition, identifier, value scheme
    or relationship produces an explicit package diff and reruns affected checks. A web
    page cannot be edited into a different meaning from the downloadable artefact.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The owning working group decides local meaning and records evidence.</li>
    <li>Interoperability reviews common-boundary terms and cross-context mappings.</li>
    <li>Vocabulary stewards control concept-scheme versions and code stability.</li>
    <li>Validation maintainers encode only rules supported by reviewed requirements.</li>
    <li>Governance controls lifecycle labels and any promotion decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="representations">Representations and projections</h2>
  <p>
    RDF/OWL represents resources and relationships; SKOS represents controlled concepts
    and taxonomies; SHACL represents declared data expectations. JSON Schema, JSON-LD,
    forms, APIs, web pages, PDF and Markdown are consumer projections. A projection must
    cite its package version, generator and validation receipt.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="One maintained semantic package is encoded in aligned graph artefacts and then transformed into consumer projections. The arrows are derivations, not transfers of semantic authority.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Semantic package representations and delivery projections
  accDescr: A governed package produces RDF and OWL, SKOS and SHACL artefacts; tested generators then produce schemas, forms and APIs, and human-readable documentation.
  Package["Governed semantic package"]:::process
  OWL["RDF / RDFS / OWL"]:::service
  SKOS["SKOS schemes"]:::data
  SHACL["SHACL shapes"]:::security
  Schema["JSON and JSON-LD"]:::infra
  Interface["Forms and APIs"]:::user
  Docs["Web, PDF and Markdown"]:::external
  Package --> OWL
  Package --> SKOS
  Package --> SHACL
  OWL --> Schema
  SKOS --> Interface
  SHACL --> Interface
  Package --> Docs
  </Diagram>
  <p><a href="/spdtf-2/outputs">Inspect the package and projection-status register</a>.</p>
</Layout>
